body:
When the Atlas complex went up in 1983 on the corner of Hoogoorddreef and Hondsrugweg, it was one of the first offices of Bullewijk, the business district meant to give Amsterdam a reachable alternative to the crowded centre. The PGGM pension fund built it purely as an investment, without knowing who would rent it; at the time it was the largest office building in the country built that way. Its architect, Frits Dekeukeleire, broke with his usual work and chose a structuralist set of interlocking volumes around two courtyards. Each part is named after a continent: Asia, Africa and America have X-shaped plans with the lifts at the crossing, so every wing can be let separately; Australia, on a lifted T, bridges between them and opens a view into the courtyard; Europe is half an X on its own. A parking garage called Antarctica followed in 1986. The landscape was designed by the office of Mien Ruys and Hans Veldhoen.

Letting was slow until Fokker Aircraft moved its head office here; when Fokker went bankrupt, tenants left again and the complex stood largely empty for years. Revived after the Arena stadium arrived next door, it is now an office campus with a hotel. The city's monuments committee called it a symbol of the 1980s leap in scale of the office market, with a largely original exterior and a rarity in the Netherlands. It became a municipal monument on 3 March 2026.

Look for:
- the strongly horizontal façades: pale precast concrete parapets with fluting and Norwegian marble chips, over dark recessed strip windows;
- the gate-like Corten steel work by Evert Strobos and the sculptures by Steef Roothaan, both made for the site;
- the green courtyards, kept free of cars by the underground parking.

Kleiburg was finished in 1971: a bent slab of about 500 rented flats, some 400 m long and ten storeys high, part of the honeycomb plan that Siegfried Nassuth of the city planning department drew for the Bijlmermeer. Like its neighbours it had a raised indoor street linked to a car park, storage boxes on the ground floor and long open galleries. In the 1980s it got cameras, sturdier doors and extra lifts, but drugs, vacancy and crime stayed. On 4 October 1992 the El Al cargo plane came down on the neighbouring flats, and Kleiburg was evacuated. A planned showpiece renovation around 2000, with lofts, escalators and a wavy new façade, was dropped for lack of money.

In 2009 housing corporation Rochdale began moving residents out to demolish it. Local campaigners pushed back, and Rochdale sold the building for a symbolic one euro to a consortium with a credible plan. The consortium, DeFlat, repaired the structure, galleries, lifts and façade, and sold the flats as bare shells (*klushuizen*, do-it-yourself homes) that buyers finished themselves, combining units if they liked. The flats were among the most affordable in Amsterdam and sold fast; by the end of 2014 the building was saved, and the renovation was finished in December 2016. In 2017 NL Architects and XVW architectuur won the EU Mies van der Rohe Award for it, the first time the prize went to the renovation of an existing building.

Look for:
- the sheer length of the bent slab, best seen from the Karspeldreef or the metro;
- the new ground floor, where the storage boxes made way for homes, studios and entrances;
- the variety of window frames and balconies chosen by individual owners.
